WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.091 --> 00:00:04.173
<v Maya>So I want you to imagine sitting in the passenger seat of a car.</v>

2
00:00:04.593 --> 00:00:04.993
<v Daniel>Okay, sure.</v>

3
00:00:05.053 --> 00:00:08.815
<v Maya>A self-driving car, right? And you are just zooming down the Las Vegas strip.</v>

4
00:00:09.195 --> 00:00:11.796
<v Daniel>Oh, nice. Neon lights. Totally smooth ride.</v>

5
00:00:11.876 --> 00:00:18.719
<v Maya>Exactly. Everything is flowing perfectly. And then, out of nowhere, the car just violently slams on the brakes.</v>

6
00:00:18.819 --> 00:00:21.601
<v Daniel>Oh, man. Yeah, your heart just completely drops into your stomach.</v>

7
00:00:21.761 --> 00:00:28.004
<v Maya>Right. You look out the windshield and there's, you know, there's no pedestrian, there's no red light, no debris in the road. It's</v>

8
00:00:28.024 --> 00:00:28.804
<v Daniel>just an empty street.</v>

9
00:00:29.055 --> 00:00:36.800
<v Maya>Yeah. And up until recently, if you asked the engineers why the car did that, you would basically just get this frustrated shrug.</v>

10
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.162
<v Daniel>Which is, I mean, it's the ultimate paradox of modern AI, isn't it?</v>

11
00:00:40.282 --> 00:00:40.802
<v Maya>Totally. We</v>

12
00:00:40.842 --> 00:00:49.908
<v Daniel>build these incredibly sophisticated systems to navigate the real world. But when they do something weird or unexpected, they act like locked black boxes.</v>

13
00:00:50.168 --> 00:00:51.930
<v Maya>Right. They take in all the sensor data.</v>

14
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:59.380
<v Daniel>Gigabytes of it. Yeah. And they do math we can't easily follow and then spit out a breaking command. When you ask it why, it's just total silence.</v>

15
00:00:59.873 --> 00:01:05.619
<v Maya>But I mean, if you are sitting in that car, trusting it with your life, silence is not an acceptable answer.</v>

16
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:06.901
<v Daniel>No, definitely not.</v>

17
00:01:07.141 --> 00:01:20.196
<v Maya>So today we're doing a deep dive into a really groundbreaking September 2026 nature paper. It's by E.O. and Kenny and a team from MIT CSAIL, along with the autonomous vehicle company Motional.</v>

18
00:01:20.503 --> 00:01:21.824
<v Daniel>It is such a cool paper.</v>

19
00:01:22.044 --> 00:01:28.268
<v Maya>It really is. We're going to unpack how they finally took that black box and basically made it talk.</v>

20
00:01:28.348 --> 00:01:31.690
<v Daniel>Yeah. They got a two ton vehicle to explain its reasoning on public roads.</v>

21
00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:37.553
<v Maya>Real public roads in real time using a vocabulary that a human driver can actually understand.</v>

22
00:01:37.693 --> 00:01:45.378
<v Daniel>Which is a massive leap, taking explainable AI off a controlled computer screen and putting it on actual asphalt at 40 miles per hour.</v>

23
00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:54.964
<v Maya>With a human safety driver inside. It's wild. But, you know, to appreciate why this is such a huge deal, we kind of have to look at the fierce debate tearing the AI community apart right now.</v>

24
00:01:55.024 --> 00:01:57.425
<v Daniel>Oh, the architectural battle over black boxes. Yes.</v>

25
00:01:57.545 --> 00:02:03.669
<v Maya>Right. It really centers on Cynthia Rudin's famous stance from back in, I think, 2019. Yeah.</v>

26
00:02:03.769 --> 00:02:06.791
<v Daniel>Her 2019 Nature Machine Intelligence paper. She came out swinging.</v>

27
00:02:07.132 --> 00:02:10.117
<v Maya>She really did. What was her main argument there again?</v>

28
00:02:10.297 --> 00:02:17.407
<v Daniel>She basically said we have to stop trying to explain black box models for high stakes decisions. Just full stop.</v>

29
00:02:17.487 --> 00:02:21.093
<v Maya>Which sounds counterintuitive. Right. Like, shouldn't we try to explain them?</v>

30
00:02:21.497 --> 00:02:29.822
<v Daniel>Well, her argument was aimed at the popular tools of the time. Stuff like WIME by Ribeiro and colleagues or SHT by Lundberg and Lee.</v>

31
00:02:30.443 --> 00:02:32.724
<v Maya>Ah, right. The post hoc explanation tools.</v>

32
00:02:32.824 --> 00:02:36.907
<v Daniel>Exactly. They generate an explanation after the AI has already made its decision.</v>

33
00:02:36.947 --> 00:02:41.350
<v Maya>I always think of those tools like an AI public relations department.</v>

34
00:02:41.548 --> 00:02:42.869
<v Daniel>That is a perfect analogy.</v>

35
00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:52.217
<v Maya>Right. Because the AI does something weird and the PR department scrambles to look at the inputs and they issue a statement saying, well, the car probably break because of that pedestrian over there.</v>

36
00:02:52.437 --> 00:02:54.478
<v Daniel>It sounds plausible. It tells a nice story.</v>

37
00:02:54.739 --> 00:03:01.524
<v Maya>But it doesn't guarantee that the pedestrian was the actual mathematical cause of the breaking. It was just a guess.</v>

38
00:03:01.664 --> 00:03:07.469
<v Daniel>It is an educated guess about the AI's internal logic. Yeah. And that's exactly what Rudin was warning us against.</v>

39
00:03:07.684 --> 00:03:11.129
<v Maya>Because a PR spin is dangerous in a high-stakes scenario.</v>

40
00:03:11.469 --> 00:03:23.166
<v Daniel>Right. For a movie recommendation, fine. But for robotic surgery or driving a car in Vegas, if the explanation isn't the literal load-bearing truth, you are being deceived.</v>

41
00:03:23.438 --> 00:03:27.620
<v Maya>She argued, we need inherently interpretable models from the ground up.</v>

42
00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:35.725
<v Daniel>Exactly. But here's the crazy thing about the Nature paper we're looking at today. Kenny's team didn't build a new model from scratch.</v>

43
00:03:35.745 --> 00:03:39.787
<v Maya>No, they didn't. They took a massive opaque black box and they wrapped it.</v>

44
00:03:40.347 --> 00:03:45.990
<v Daniel>Which sounds like they're just ignoring Rudin's warning. But they actually found this brilliant middle path.</v>

45
00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.592
<v Maya>Yeah, they built something called the Concept Wrapper Network, or CWNet.</v>

46
00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:55.504
<v Daniel>Taking inspiration from a 2023 paper by Yuk Sekenal and others on post hoc concept bottleneck models.</v>

47
00:03:55.744 --> 00:04:02.029
<v Maya>So they took an existing black box and forced it to be inherently interpretable. Let's pop the hood on that original black box.</v>

48
00:04:02.049 --> 00:04:03.571
<v Daniel>Yeah, let's talk about Drive RL.</v>

49
00:04:03.631 --> 00:04:06.553
<v Maya>Drive RL. It's an inverse reinforcement learning planner.</v>

50
00:04:06.593 --> 00:04:12.998
<v Daniel>Right. And to break that down, standard reinforcement learning gives an AI a score to maximize. Like a video game.</v>

51
00:04:13.138 --> 00:04:14.599
<v Maya>You get points for not crashing.</v>

52
00:04:14.919 --> 00:04:23.976
<v Daniel>Exactly. But inverse reinforcement learning works backward. They fed this AI roughly 80 hours of driving footage from human experts.</v>

53
00:04:24.056 --> 00:04:25.678
<v Maya>So it just watches humans drive.</v>

54
00:04:25.718 --> 00:04:30.144
<v Daniel>Yeah. And its job is to figure out the hidden rules the human was trying to maximize.</v>

55
00:04:30.384 --> 00:04:33.308
<v Maya>Like inferring the unspoken rules of the road.</v>

56
00:04:33.748 --> 00:04:38.234
<v Daniel>Exactly. Like, oh, the human always slows down when the lane gets narrow, so that must be a rule.</v>

57
00:04:38.434 --> 00:04:41.878
<v Maya>And it uses a really complex scene encoder to do this, right?</v>

58
00:04:41.898 --> 00:04:51.971
<v Daniel>A hierarchical vector transformer. Instead of just looking at the road as flat pixels, it translates physical elements into math. Lane lines, crosswalks, moving cars.</v>

59
00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:57.891
<v Maya>So it's constantly calculating that, you know, car A is moving at a certain speed relative to pedestrian B.</v>

60
00:04:58.111 --> 00:05:05.073
<v Daniel>Right. And based on that math, the trajectory generator proposes roughly 146 different candidate paths the car could take.</v>

61
00:05:05.193 --> 00:05:06.253
<v Maya>Every single step.</v>

62
00:05:06.453 --> 00:05:13.974
<v Daniel>Every single step. Fraction as a second. In its original form, DriveIRL evaluates all those paths and a final, completely opaque reward layer.</v>

63
00:05:14.094 --> 00:05:15.875
<v Maya>It just picks the winner and we have no idea why.</v>

64
00:05:15.995 --> 00:05:16.375
<v Daniel>None at all.</v>

65
00:05:16.695 --> 00:05:22.280
<v Maya>So how did Kenny's team fix that without throwing the whole model away? Because retraining it would cost a fortune.</v>

66
00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:29.026
<v Daniel>It would take an eternity. So they did this fascinating architectural trick. They basically sliced off that final opaque reward layer.</v>

67
00:05:29.066 --> 00:05:30.247
<v Maya>Just chopped it right off.</v>

68
00:05:30.427 --> 00:05:33.810
<v Daniel>Yep. And in its place, they inserted a concept classifier.</v>

69
00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:34.711
<v Maya>Okay, what does that do?</v>

70
00:05:34.951 --> 00:05:40.755
<v Daniel>It acts as a filter. It looks at the scene and scores human-readable concepts. concrete things.</v>

71
00:05:40.995 --> 00:05:41.636
<v Maya>What kind of things?</v>

72
00:05:41.756 --> 00:05:50.139
<v Daniel>Things like close, meaning the car is within three meters of another vehicle. Or ASV, which is approaching stopped vehicle.</v>

73
00:05:50.299 --> 00:05:52.780
<v Maya>Okay, so things a human driver actually thinks about.</v>

74
00:05:52.820 --> 00:06:00.083
<v Daniel>Exactly. They also have bike, pedestrian, intersection, stop, slow, fast, and pickup zones, like Quito.</v>

75
00:06:00.243 --> 00:06:05.724
<v Maya>And they train this classifier on a ton of data, right? Like 500,000 scenarios just for eight concepts. Yeah,</v>

76
00:06:05.844 --> 00:06:10.346
<v Daniel>up to 3 million scenarios for 10 concepts. But here is the critical part of CWNet.</v>

77
00:06:10.546 --> 00:06:11.646
<v Maya>Right, the new reward layer.</v>

78
00:06:11.726 --> 00:06:19.488
<v Daniel>Yes. They add a brand new final reward layer on top. And this new layer is completely blind to all the complex vector math from before.</v>

79
00:06:19.809 --> 00:06:22.091
<v Maya>Wait, really? I can't see the original math.</v>

80
00:06:22.191 --> 00:06:26.476
<v Daniel>Nope. The only thing it is allowed to look at are those human readable concept scores.</v>

81
00:06:26.676 --> 00:06:30.780
<v Maya>Wow. So that structural bottleneck is how they answer Cynthia Rudin.</v>

82
00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:39.749
<v Daniel>Exactly. Because the final decision routes exclusively through these human concepts. The explanation is causally faithful by construction.</v>

83
00:06:40.029 --> 00:06:42.071
<v Maya>It reminds me of a corporate middle manager.</v>

84
00:06:42.531 --> 00:06:42.972
<v Daniel>How so?</v>

85
00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:50.278
<v Maya>Well, imagine a manager who has to write down a checklist of concrete reasons before they approve a budget. They can't just say, my gut told</v>

86
00:06:50.318 --> 00:06:51.640
<v Daniel>me to. They have to show their work.</v>

87
00:06:51.865 --> 00:06:59.691
<v Maya>They have to physically check the box that says approaching stopped vehicle. So if the car breaks, it is irrefutably because that box was checked.</v>

88
00:06:59.832 --> 00:07:07.037
<v Daniel>It is the load-bearing truth. And here's the kicker. Yeah. Usually when you force an AI to be transparent, you pay a heavy tax on performance.</v>

89
00:07:07.218 --> 00:07:08.218
<v Maya>The model gets dumber.</v>

90
00:07:08.298 --> 00:07:15.724
<v Daniel>Right. But on the massive new plan benchmark, wrapping this black box cost essentially zero performance.</v>

91
00:07:15.825 --> 00:07:17.446
<v Maya>Are you serious? Zero.</v>

92
00:07:17.666 --> 00:07:23.312
<v Daniel>It performed within 1% of the original opaque model. The interpretability was basically free.</v>

93
00:07:23.673 --> 00:07:27.637
<v Maya>That's incredible. But I mean, the math working on a benchmark data set is one thing.</v>

94
00:07:28.037 --> 00:07:29.599
<v Daniel>The real world is much messier.</v>

95
00:07:29.839 --> 00:07:36.647
<v Maya>Exactly. And that's the heart of this deep dive. They actually put human safety drivers in this car on a private track.</v>

96
00:07:36.942 --> 00:07:45.907
<v Daniel>Yes, they did. And they documented three specific road stories that show how this alters a human's mental model in real time.</v>

97
00:07:46.128 --> 00:07:49.830
<v Maya>Let's talk about the first story, the pickup zone and the parked cars.</v>

98
00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:56.734
<v Daniel>Yes. So the driver's on the track and the car keeps getting stuck near a pickup and drop off zone, a PUD.</v>

99
00:07:57.074 --> 00:08:03.378
<v Maya>And the human driver just assumes the zone itself is the problem, right? Like maybe the map data is confusing the car.</v>

100
00:08:03.776 --> 00:08:09.522
<v Daniel>Perfectly logical assumption. If this were a black box, they'd just file a bug report saying it's broken at PUD zones.</v>

101
00:08:09.682 --> 00:08:12.565
<v Maya>But they had the CWNet explanations on the dashboard.</v>

102
00:08:12.746 --> 00:08:18.632
<v Daniel>And the car wasn't confused by the pickup zone at all. The explanation showed the close concept was firing off the charts.</v>

103
00:08:18.852 --> 00:08:21.315
<v Maya>Because it was looking at parked cars nearby and panicking.</v>

104
00:08:21.633 --> 00:08:30.999
<v Daniel>Exactly. It thought it was too close to them. So the driver physically takes the wheel, moves the car slightly away from the parked cars, and turns autonomous mode back on.</v>

105
00:08:31.139 --> 00:08:32.740
<v Maya>And it just starts moving perfectly.</v>

106
00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:36.182
<v Daniel>Instantly. The close probability drops, and off it goes.</v>

107
00:08:36.583 --> 00:08:45.088
<v Maya>And the data backs this up. Across over 5,500 samples, the close concept correlated with speed at a Pearson R of about minus 0.56.</v>

108
00:08:45.789 --> 00:08:47.950
<v Daniel>When close goes up, speed goes down.</v>

109
00:08:48.363 --> 00:08:54.567
<v Maya>So the explanation instantly corrected a faulty human assumption. They debugged the situation on the fly.</v>

110
00:08:54.708 --> 00:09:00.452
<v Daniel>Right. But that was for real, physical cars. What happens when the sensors react to something that isn't there?</v>

111
00:09:01.172 --> 00:09:04.775
<v Maya>Ah, the second story. The traffic cone hallucination.</v>

112
00:09:05.015 --> 00:09:10.319
<v Daniel>The car is driving along and inexplicably slams on the brakes next to a standard traffic cone.</v>

113
00:09:10.459 --> 00:09:14.762
<v Maya>And again, the driver blames the obvious thing. They assume the car doesn't know how to handle a cone.</v>

114
00:09:15.123 --> 00:09:21.047
<v Daniel>The explanation shows the ASV concept approaching stopped vehicle is just spiking massively.</v>

115
00:09:21.147 --> 00:09:23.449
<v Maya>So it thinks the cone is a full-sized parked car?</v>

116
00:09:23.589 --> 00:09:26.151
<v Daniel>It believes it's approaching a vehicle that does not exist.</v>

117
00:09:26.271 --> 00:09:28.312
<v Maya>And they proved it with a counterfactual test?</v>

118
00:09:28.612 --> 00:09:34.136
<v Daniel>Yes. They removed the cone, drove the exact same road, and the car braked identically.</v>

119
00:09:34.377 --> 00:09:37.058
<v Maya>Wow. With the exact same ASV spike.</v>

120
00:09:37.299 --> 00:09:40.781
<v Daniel>Exactly. The hallucination had absolutely nothing to do with the cone.</v>

121
00:09:40.905 --> 00:09:46.447
<v Maya>That is profound. AI hallucinations are so hard to pin down. If you were in a black box, you'd just be terrified.</v>

122
00:09:46.667 --> 00:09:52.409
<v Daniel>But CWNet made the phantom visible. The driver could see the ghost the car was braking for.</v>

123
00:09:52.629 --> 00:09:56.350
<v Maya>That's wild. But the third story is the most honest one in the paper, I think.</v>

124
00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:57.410
<v Daniel>The cyclist paradox.</v>

125
00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:02.112
<v Maya>Right. So the car approaches the cyclist, and thankfully, it successfully stops.</v>

126
00:10:02.387 --> 00:10:03.328
<v Daniel>Which sounds like a win.</v>

127
00:10:03.648 --> 00:10:10.213
<v Maya>It does. But the engineers look at the dashboard, and the bike concept stayed under 1% the whole time.</v>

128
00:10:10.453 --> 00:10:13.616
<v Daniel>CWNet completely failed to register the cyclist.</v>

129
00:10:13.736 --> 00:10:15.057
<v Maya>So why did it stop?</v>

130
00:10:15.117 --> 00:10:20.962
<v Daniel>Well, they investigated. The car's raw perception system, the cameras and LIDARs, did see the bike.</v>

131
00:10:21.242 --> 00:10:22.583
<v Maya>Okay, so the eyes worked.</v>

132
00:10:22.703 --> 00:10:28.768
<v Daniel>Yes. But the drive IRL planner, the brain, was never wired to consume those cyclist inputs.</v>

133
00:10:28.988 --> 00:10:30.649
<v Maya>It was essentially ignoring the cyclist.</v>

134
00:10:30.709 --> 00:10:32.810
<v Daniel>Totally. It was on a trajectory to collide.</v>

135
00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:34.011
<v Maya>So what stopped it?</v>

136
00:10:34.271 --> 00:10:40.235
<v Daniel>A completely separate, hard-coded emergency backup system kicked in at the last second and hit the brakes.</v>

137
00:10:40.475 --> 00:10:44.197
<v Maya>Oh, wow. So the explanation system showed a total failure of the planner.</v>

138
00:10:44.498 --> 00:10:50.361
<v Daniel>And that is the paradox. A completely inaccurate explanation was the most valuable piece of information they got.</v>

139
00:10:50.521 --> 00:10:57.576
<v Maya>Because it exposed a massive blind spot. If they didn't have the dashboard, the driver would think... cool the ai handles cyclists perfectly</v>

140
00:10:57.816 --> 00:11:02.419
<v Daniel>but because bikes stayed at zero they realized the core planner was dangerously blind</v>

141
00:11:02.999 --> 00:11:07.442
<v Maya>the authors actually argue that low accuracy concepts are off the valuable ones right</v>

142
00:11:07.642 --> 00:11:16.968
<v Daniel>yeah because knowing when your ai is blind is just as important as knowing what it sees the safety drivers started approaching cyclists way more cautiously after that</v>

143
00:11:17.208 --> 00:11:21.931
<v Maya>it fundamentally shifted their mental model but you know those are just trap stories anecdotes</v>

144
00:11:22.051 --> 00:11:26.974
<v Daniel>right does this actually scale Does it measurably improve situational awareness?</v>

145
00:11:27.154 --> 00:11:32.017
<v Maya>To answer that, they ran rigorous human studies, starting with some online replications.</v>

146
00:11:32.157 --> 00:11:36.140
<v Daniel>Yeah, they tested nine emotional experts and 30 non-experts on Prolific.</v>

147
00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:44.346
<v Maya>And the results were pretty stark. Mental models improved for eight out of nine experts and 27 out of 30 non-experts.</v>

148
00:11:44.446 --> 00:11:48.528
<v Daniel>And that improvement directly predicted how accurately people could guess the car's next move.</v>

149
00:11:48.989 --> 00:11:52.111
<v Maya>But the real test was the Las Vegas public roads study.</v>

150
00:11:52.505 --> 00:11:56.648
<v Daniel>Oh, this was intense. A hundred participants out in the chaos of the Vegas Strip. And</v>

151
00:11:56.668 --> 00:11:57.769
<v Maya>they used SAGIT, right?</v>

152
00:11:57.969 --> 00:12:02.973
<v Daniel>Yeah, the Situation Awareness Global Assessment Technique. It's usually used for fighter pilots.</v>

153
00:12:03.153 --> 00:12:04.955
<v Maya>To see if they're getting overwhelmed in the cockpit.</v>

154
00:12:05.175 --> 00:12:14.742
<v Daniel>Exactly. They adapted it to see how well humans understand the dynamic driving environment, comparing CWNet against a control group that only saw speed and steering data.</v>

155
00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:17.241
<v Maya>And the results on surprising events were huge.</v>

156
00:12:17.521 --> 00:12:27.644
<v Daniel>Massive effect sizes. For perception of the environment, Cohen's d was about 1.29. That's huge. For comprehension, 1.00. For projection, 0.61.</v>

157
00:12:27.864 --> 00:12:33.525
<v Maya>To put that in perspective for you listening, an effect size of 1.29 is the difference between panic and calm understanding.</v>

158
00:12:33.625 --> 00:12:36.566
<v Daniel>It really is. But there was also a really counterintuitive finding.</v>

159
00:12:36.913 --> 00:12:43.598
<v Maya>Right. On ordinary, unsurprising driving, there was no significant difference whether they had the explanations or not.</v>

160
00:12:43.798 --> 00:12:44.239
<v Daniel>Not at all.</v>

161
00:12:44.659 --> 00:12:51.744
<v Maya>Which, I mean, I have to push back on. If I buy an expensive, explainable car and it does nothing during my normal commute, I'd feel like it's broken.</v>

162
00:12:52.285 --> 00:12:56.328
<v Daniel>You'd think so. But the authors frame this as the ultimate safety feature.</v>

163
00:12:56.668 --> 00:12:57.449
<v Maya>How so?</v>

164
00:12:57.749 --> 00:13:04.454
<v Daniel>Well, think about cognitive clutter. If the dashboard is constantly screaming explanations at you when everything is fine, you get fatigued.</v>

165
00:13:04.969 --> 00:13:06.330
<v Maya>Ah, you just start tuning it out.</v>

166
00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:11.074
<v Daniel>Exactly. It fades into the background normally and only commands attention when things get weird.</v>

167
00:13:11.515 --> 00:13:15.698
<v Maya>Which makes sense. And that concept classifier itself wasn't even perfect, right?</v>

168
00:13:15.978 --> 00:13:20.702
<v Daniel>Deliberately imperfect. Its mean accuracy was only around 0.54. Wow,</v>

169
00:13:20.782 --> 00:13:22.203
<v Maya>so just over 50%.</v>

170
00:13:22.383 --> 00:13:28.088
<v Daniel>It proves you don't need a mathematically flawless AI to vastly improve a human's mental model.</v>

171
00:13:28.248 --> 00:13:29.770
<v Maya>As long as it's causally faithful.</v>

172
00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:30.510
<v Daniel>Exactly. Which</v>

173
00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:33.893
<v Maya>brings us to the broader implications, because this isn't just about cars.</v>

174
00:13:34.248 --> 00:13:43.032
<v Daniel>No, the authors say this causal wrapper method can apply to autonomous drones, robotic surgeons, vision language action models.</v>

175
00:13:43.432 --> 00:13:52.535
<v Maya>Imagine a robotic surgeon making an unexpected cut. You want a causally faithful checklist showing exactly why it did that, not a post hoc PR guess.</v>

176
00:13:53.036 --> 00:13:58.538
<v Daniel>And regulators are catching up. In the EU and U.S., explainability is being written into law.</v>

177
00:13:59.039 --> 00:14:03.700
<v Maya>So this won't just be a neat research trick. It might be a strict legal requirement to put a robot in public.</v>

178
00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:04.460
<v Daniel>Very soon, yes.</v>

179
00:14:04.580 --> 00:14:09.161
<v Maya>But, you know, we have to leave you with the open question here. This was one system.</v>

180
00:14:09.581 --> 00:14:10.602
<v Daniel>One company's planner.</v>

181
00:14:10.702 --> 00:14:17.343
<v Maya>Right. Tested by a small pool of experts using concepts a human had to hand label in advance, like bike or stop.</v>

182
00:14:17.483 --> 00:14:20.284
<v Daniel>And the real world has an infinite, messy vocabulary.</v>

183
00:14:20.404 --> 00:14:26.185
<v Maya>Exactly. Does hand labeling concepts scale to the full chaos of the road? What if a mattress falls off a truck?</v>

184
00:14:26.346 --> 00:14:30.573
<v Daniel>Or a flock of wild turkeys crosses the highway? Did anyone label a turkey concept?</v>

185
00:14:30.834 --> 00:14:36.644
<v Maya>Right. What happens when a self-driving car makes a fatal error based on a concept no human ever thought to label?</v>

186
00:14:36.744 --> 00:14:38.647
<v Daniel>The dashboard would just be completely blank.</v>

187
00:14:38.928 --> 00:14:47.162
<v Maya>Because the AI is reacting to a ghost we never gave a name to, it is definitely something to chew on the next time you let a machine take the wheel.</v>
